  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nToday into this evening...Scattered clouds and some fog (locally\ndense) early will give way to mostly sunny skies in the afternoon\nas we will be north of front that is moving through early this \nmorning. A couple CAMS hint that if some elevated showers form in\neastern SD this morning, they could drift over our southern areas\nthis afternoon. Something to watch, but most guidance keeps us \ndry, so will ride with that idea. Dry weather and more sun than \nSunday will boost temps into the lower to middle 80s, though east \ncomponent winds will keep Lake Michigan shore/Door County cooler. \nDry weather holds for any evening activities, though patchy fog \nmay occur near Lake Michigan. Temperatures will be in the low to \nmid 70s. \n\nShower and storm chances (15-30%) return late tonight through \nWednesday...Showers and storms developing this evening over \nDakotas forecast to produce shortwave/upper jet that shifts east \ntoward WI late tonight. Gradient of instability from MN to \nsouthern WI, as is the stronger convergence with LLJ. Even so, \nwith the wave and elevated instability farther north, cannot rule \nout some showers and storms on Tuesday morning, with perhaps \nslight boost in coverage farther north during peak heating of \nafternoon as MLCAPEs increase over 1000J/kg. Effective shear over \n40 kts would support stronger storm if convection develops. SPC \ndoes keep the marginal risk for severe farther south near the more\npersistent frontal boundary/highest instability. \n\nRain chances (30-60%) then continue Tuesday night into Wednesday \nas additional as another shortwave moves through the region and \nthe front remains close enough to our area. Similar to expectations\nfor late tonight and Tuesday, appears better signal for severe \nremains south. However, late Tuesday night through Wednesday, \nthere is stronger forcing farther north and sufficient instability.\nSeems like this time frame may hold the greatest chances for some\nstronger storms over our area, especially if things slow a bit and\nthese factors catch up to at least the beginning of peak heating \non Wed. FWIW, ML progs show a weak \"bump\" in severe probs to 5-15% \nfor Wed after a relative minimum in severe potential Mon-Tue.\n\nDry and cooler late this week, then chances for rain (30-40%) return\nfor the weekend...High pressure builds in across the area Wednesday\nnight through Friday, pushing the front and lingering instability\nsouth and generally keeping the area dry through the rest of the \nweek. Highs will settle into the 70s regionwide. Low temperatures \nin the 40s north seem a certainty Thursday night with high overhead.\nLow end from NBM (5th percentile) even points to potential for \nupper 30s in typical cool spots near the U.P. border (e.g. KLNL). \n\nThe high eventually shifts east, allowing for the chance for showers\nand thunderstorms to return to the area Friday night into next weekend.\nOn the early side, but does seem that Sunday would be the drier of the\ntwo days.",
